A||||8||Scott2M4||Will Grand Am radio fit Fiero?||||5
Z||000000||Scott2M4||11-14-1999||10:42 AM||||Found a Delco cassette deck out of a 1995 Grand Am on Ebay this morning.  Will it fit my 1987 coupe?<P>P.S.  I'm assuming, since it came out of a Grand Am, that is has the orange illumination.  Is this a correct assumption?<P>P.S.S.  I've provided the Ebay link that shows the front and back of the unit:<BR> <A HREF="http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=198655450" TARGET=_blank>http://cgi.ebay.com/aw-cgi/eBayISAPI.dll?ViewItem&item=198655450</A> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Scott2M4||11-14-1999||10:43 AM||||P.S.S.S.  I forgot to mention that I have the base(?) Delco AM/FM radio in it now.||||reg||4
Z||000002||Formula||11-14-1999||02:25 PM||||It will fit physically, you might need a wire adapter kit, hmmmmmmm...... 2 kits, one that fits the back of the stereo and then one that hooks into the factory harness, then splice them together with bullet plugs.<BR>I imagine all gm car delco stereos have the screw holes. any more suggestions... ||||reg||1
Z||000003||batboy||11-14-1999||02:42 PM||||I see those Delco radios all the time at swap meets, usually selling dirt cheap because so many people install aftermarket stereos. I personally don't know much about this factory radio swap stuff, but I'm referring to a FOCOA article called "Tune-up!" that was in their 2nd quarter 1999 magazine issue.<P>Sounds like that eBay radio will physically fit although it will be a very tight fit, but here are some cautions that I gleamed from the article. Note that all the plug adapters and wiring may cost upwards to $40 or more, so I'm not sure this is a great bargain unless you can get the radio really cheap.<P>1) 1994 or newer stereos are the best GM Delco radios.<P>2) Use only the Pontiac version, other GM versions (for example: Camaro) use different color codes on their wiring harness.<P>3) Have the stereo tested before you buy.<P>4) 1994 and newer Delco stereos have a a feature called "Theftlock." If the radio was removed before the Theftlock was disabled, and you don't have the correct code, then the radio is useless.<P>5) The antenna plug needs an adapter to work (different size plugs).<P>6) You have to use a radio wiring harness adapter plug or snag the harness from the donor car and modify it slightly.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Kage86GT4SP||11-15-1999||01:33 AM||||actually, I don't believe that the radio in question came out of a 95 anything.<P>That is an exact duplicate of the radio that came out of my 86 SE, to include the plug in.<P>The price speaks for itself, assuming the deck works.  If it doesn't, then it's probably the rubber band that moves the capstans in the deck.  Easy to replace, and not too expensive.  ($15 or so)||||reg||1
Z||000005||RAT||11-17-1999||05:22 PM||||in a 95 the wireing harnist is diferent you would need to get 80 to 91  gm wireing harnist <BR>IF you want a cheap cd player that will do just as it says i bought the really cheap version from wall mart almost a year ago it was aproximitly 116 with space adapter and wireing harnist (it was really easy to install)<BR>it ia called Road Gear 60 watt am/fm cd player i did not even have to change speakers||||reg||3
Z||000006||CUTTER||11-18-1999||10:25 PM||||If you want a AC-Delco that you can just pop in your car and hook it up in seconds with out changing wires or anything, you have to get one out of a pontiac in the years of 84 to maybe around 89,90. they will pop right in. If you go any newer, you might have to change the wiring harness, and the antenna plug thingy.||||reg||1
Z||000007||DJRice||11-19-1999||09:13 AM||||The radios from most other GM lines will fit as well. ||||reg||1
Z||000008||Standard||11-20-1999||03:23 AM||||See Batboy's post, #2.  Different divisions use different color codes for the wiring.  I'm sure you could use it but it would be a headache figuring out which wire goes where..||||reg||4
